**Current versus future.** `export:` is the implemented public API marker.
The later `$export` migration will replace it. Its directive-block syntax is
undecided, and the two forms won't remain permanent alternatives.

Declarations are private to their module by default. The `export:` block marks
what other modules can see.

### A compact export block

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    private_helper || -> String:
        return "private"
    ;

    export:
        public_name #= "Moth"

        render || -> String:
            return private_helper()
        ;
    ;
]

### Private by default

Files in the same module can see each other's declarations. Other modules can
only see what the root's `export:` block makes public. The `#` marker means
compile-time constant, not public visibility.

Private functions without an error slot can propagate implicit numeric failure
within their module without postfix `!` at each internal call. Exported
callables without an error slot must handle that failure, not silently add an
error channel. Built-in `Error!` can deliver it; custom errors need recovery or
explicit mapping. Traits and generic conformance cannot widen the public
signature. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

Re-export direct selections inside the same current block. Don't use `$export`
until its migration and syntax land.
